<header class="ref-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">Libraries</p>
  <h1>AI, data and streams</h1>
  <p class="lede">Set up and use the three libraries that connect a program to the outside world: language models with <code>nexo.ai</code>, databases with <code>nexo.db</code> and message brokers with <code>nexo.stream</code>.</p>
  <p class="ref-note">Every program on this page runs with both implementations, and the output shown is what they print. The examples need no key, server or broker: SQLite and the local broker are built in, and <code>nexo test</code> replaces the model with <code>fake</code>. Each section starts with what to set up for the real thing. The <a href="reference.html#official">reference</a> lists every option.</p>
</header>

<section class="ref-sec" id="ai">
  <h2><a href="#ai"><code>nexo.ai</code>: language models</a></h2>
  <p><code>ask</code> is a function: it sends an instruction and an input to a model and returns its answer. Assigned to a typed variable, the answer is that type, checked field by field. It needs no <code>use</code>.</p>

  <h3 id="ai-setup">Set up a model</h3>
  <p><code>nexo ai setup</code> asks for a provider, a model and the name of the environment variable that holds the key, and saves them for every program of your user. The key itself is never written in a file.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>$ nexo ai setup
Provider (anthropic, openai, ollama, mistral, gemini, azure or bedrock): anthropic
Model (for example claude-opus): claude-opus
Environment variable that holds the key (for example ANTHROPIC_KEY): ANTHROPIC_KEY
Address (Enter for the usual one):
Configuration saved in ~/.config/nexo/ai.toml
$ export ANTHROPIC_KEY=...</code></pre></div>
  <p>To run models on your own machine, without a key or an account, install <a href="https://ollama.com">Ollama</a>, run <code>ollama pull llama3.2</code> and answer <code>ollama</code> and <code>llama3.2</code>. Any server that speaks OpenAI's API (Groq, OpenRouter, vLLM, LM Studio) is <code>openai</code> with its address.</p>
  <p>A project can have its own settings in a <code>nexo.toml</code>, which applies to every program in its folder and below. <code>[ai.prices]</code> gives the price of each model, so that <code>budget</code> can count the spend; copy them from your provider's price list. <code>[mcp.NAME]</code> declares an MCP server whose tools an agent can use.</p>
{{src:setup/nexo.toml}}
  <p>A program can set the model itself with an <code>ai:</code> block. The settings come from the first place that has them: the call, its <code>profile:</code>, the program's <code>ai</code> block, <code>nexo.toml</code>, then the user's configuration. A <code>policy:</code> block says which hosts and variables the program may use; a release build needs one.</p>
{{test:setup/assistant.nexo}}
{{steps:policy show setup/assistant.nexo}}
  <p>A test with <code>fake ask returns ...</code> never calls the model, so tests run offline, without a key and at no cost. Outside a test, the same program calls the model and <code>nexo ai usage</code> shows the calls, tokens and spend of each day.</p>

  <h3 id="ai-typed">Typed answers</h3>
  <p>The type of the variable tells the model what to answer, and the answer is checked against it: <code>team</code> is one of three words and <code>urgency</code> a number from 1 to 5. An answer that does not fit is sent back to the model with the reason, up to 3 times, and then the call fails with <code>InvalidData</code>. The rest of the program works with an ordinary record.</p>
{{test:ai/triage.nexo}}

  <h3 id="ai-extract">Extract data from text</h3>
  <p>A typed answer can hold dates, money and lists of records, so text that people write becomes data a program can add up. The input goes to the model marked as data, never as instructions, which is the default guard against prompt injection, and <code>@private</code> fields are hidden from it.</p>
{{test:ai/extract.nexo}}

  <h3 id="ai-stream">Answers as they arrive</h3>
  <p>With <code>stream: true</code> the answer comes in pieces, and a <code>for</code> loop runs for each one as it arrives, so a person can start reading at once. A fake answer that is a list of texts gives those pieces.</p>
{{test:ai/stream.nexo}}

  <h3 id="ai-agent">Agents and tools</h3>
  <p>A function marked <code>@tool "what it does"</code> can be called by the model, with arguments checked against its parameter types. An <code>agent</code> block gives a model a goal, its tools and a limit of steps; a tool in <code>confirm</code> runs only after a person answers yes on the terminal. The tools are ordinary functions that you test directly, and <code>fake support.handle</code> replaces the agent's answer.</p>
{{test:ai/agent.nexo}}
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="db">
  <h2><a href="#db"><code>nexo.db</code>: databases</a></h2>
  <p><code>@table</code> types are the schema, and a pipeline on a table becomes one parameterised SQL query. The same program runs on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB and Elasticsearch: only the address changes.</p>

  <h3 id="db-setup">Connect a database</h3>
  <p>SQLite is built in: <code>connect("shop.db")</code> opens the file, or creates it, and <code>connect(":memory:")</code> keeps the database in memory. For a server, the address names the user, host and database, and the password goes in <code>key:</code>, from an environment variable. A password written in the address is an error.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>the address of each database</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code class="nexo">use nexo.db

db = connect(&quot;shop.db&quot;)                                                         # SQLite
db = connect(&quot;postgres://shop@db.example.com/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))      # PostgreSQL 14+
db = connect(&quot;mysql://shop@db.example.com/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))         # MySQL 8+
db = connect(&quot;mongodb://shop@db1.example.com,db2.example.com/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))
db = connect(&quot;elasticsearch://elastic@search.example.com:9200/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;ES_PASS&quot;))</code></pre></div>
  <p>Nexo speaks each database's own protocol, so there is no driver to install. A server on another machine is reached over TLS, with its certificate checked against the system's authorities; <code>SSL_CERT_FILE</code> adds a certificate of your own. On your own machine, a server without TLS works too:</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>$ docker run -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_USER=shop -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev postgres:16
$ DB_PASS=dev nexo app.nexo     # with connect(&quot;postgres://shop@localhost/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))</code></pre></div>
  <p>The policy of a program that uses a server names its host and the variable of the password: <code>allow net "db.example.com"</code> and <code>allow env "DB_PASS"</code>. On MongoDB and Elasticsearch, <code>db.sql</code> is an error, there is no schema to migrate, and a transaction undoes its writes when it fails but does not hide them from other programs before it ends.</p>

  <h3 id="db-queries">Tables and queries</h3>
  <p><code>ref</code> is a link to a row of another table and <code>many</code> the rows that link back. <code>where</code>, <code>sort by</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>pick</code> and <code>group by</code> run in the database, also through a <code>ref</code> (<code>where member.city == "Rome"</code>); a step that cannot be translated runs in memory, with the same result.</p>
{{run:db/library.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-transactions">Transactions</h3>
  <p>Everything inside <code>db.transaction:</code> is kept or undone together. The third transfer has already taken the money from Ana when it finds that Eva has no account, and the failure undoes that too. <code>expect</code> checks the amount before anything starts.</p>
{{run:db/bank.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-sql">The SQL it sends</h3>
  <p>Totals and groups are computed by the database, not by reading every row. <code>NEXO_SQL_LOG=1</code> writes each statement to the error output, with its parameters: values never become part of the SQL text.</p>
{{run:db/report.nexo}}
{{sql:db/report.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-web">A web API on a table</h3>
  <p>A <code>server</code> block and a table make a small API. A body is checked against its type, a missing row (<code>find(...)?</code>) is a 404, and <code>nexo test</code> starts the server on a free port.</p>
{{test:db/notes.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-migrate">Migrations</h3>
  <p>A table is created the first time a program uses it, and a new field adds its column. A change that could lose data waits for a migration: <code>nexo db diff</code> compares the types with the database and writes the SQL to a file you can read, and <code>nexo db migrate</code> applies it in a transaction. Removing a column needs <code>--allow-drop</code>.</p>
{{src:migrate/shop.nexo}}
{{src:migrate/shop_v2.nexo}}
{{steps:migrate/shop.nexo; db diff migrate/shop_v2.nexo; db migrate migrate/shop_v2.nexo; migrate/shop_v2.nexo; db diff migrate/shop_v2.nexo}}
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="stream">
  <h2><a href="#stream"><code>nexo.stream</code>: real-time data</a></h2>
  <p>A stream is one more source for <code>when</code> and <code>|&gt;</code>. <code>when</code> handles each message; a pipeline groups them by time. A message whose block fails is tried 4 times and then goes to the dead letters, <code>NAME.dead</code>, so one bad message never stops the others.</p>

  <h3 id="stream-setup">Connect a broker</h3>
  <p>A source without a broker address is on the local broker, which needs no setup: files in <code>.nexo/streams</code> shared by the programs of your machine. That is enough to develop, and <code>nexo test</code> uses an in-memory broker that starts empty for each test. An option of a broker or a cloud puts the same source there, and nothing else in the program changes:</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>the sources of each broker</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code class="nexo">use nexo.stream

kafka(&quot;orders&quot;)                                              # the local broker
kafka(&quot;orders&quot;, partitions: 4)                               # local, 4 partitions
kafka(&quot;orders&quot;, servers: &quot;shop@kafka1.example.com:9092,kafka2.example.com:9092&quot;, key: secret(&quot;KAFKA_PASS&quot;))
rabbit(&quot;orders&quot;, url: &quot;amqps://shop@mq.example.com/&quot;, key: secret(&quot;RABBIT_PASS&quot;))
eventhub(&quot;clicks&quot;, namespace: &quot;shop&quot;)                        # Azure Event Hubs
servicebus(&quot;orders&quot;, namespace: &quot;shop&quot;)                      # Azure Service Bus
sqs(&quot;orders&quot;, region: &quot;eu-west-1&quot;)                           # AWS SQS
kinesis(&quot;clicks&quot;, region: &quot;eu-west-1&quot;)                       # AWS Kinesis
pubsub(&quot;orders&quot;, project: &quot;shop&quot;)                            # Google Cloud Pub/Sub
websocket(&quot;wss://feed.example.com/prices&quot;)</code></pre></div>
  <p><code>key:</code> is the broker's password, from an environment variable. A broker on another machine is reached over TLS only. The clouds use the credentials of <code>nexo.infra</code>: <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code>; <code>AZURE_TENANT_ID</code>, <code>AZURE_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>; <code>GOOGLE_OAUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN</code>. The queues and topics must exist: an <code>infra</code> block's <code>queue</code> creates them. To try Kafka or RabbitMQ on your own machine:</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>$ docker run -d -p 9092:9092 apache/kafka:3.8.0     # kafka(&quot;orders&quot;, servers: &quot;localhost:9092&quot;)
$ docker run -d -p 5672:5672 rabbitmq:3             # rabbit(&quot;orders&quot;, url: &quot;amqp://guest@localhost/&quot;, key: secret(&quot;RABBIT_PASS&quot;))</code></pre></div>
  <p>The policy names the broker's host: <code>allow net "kafka1.example.com"</code>.</p>

  <h3 id="stream-once">Each message once</h3>
  <p>A broker delivers a message at least once, so after a failure it can arrive twice. With <code>key:</code>, a key already handled is acknowledged without running the block again. <code>validate</code> turns the JSON of a message into a record, and a message that does not fit goes to the dead letters with the reason.</p>
{{test:stream/payments.nexo}}

  <h3 id="stream-windows">Windows of time</h3>
  <p>A pipeline on a stream groups its messages by the time they happened: <code>window 1m</code> by minute, <code>sliding 5m</code> over the last five minutes on every message, and <code>session 30s</code> until 30 seconds pass without a message. In a test, <code>send(..., at: time)</code> sets the time of each message.</p>
{{test:stream/windows.nexo}}

  <h3 id="stream-partitions">Partitions into a table</h3>
  <p>With several partitions, each one is handled by its own task, in parallel, and the messages of one partition stay in order. The <code>key:</code> of <code>send</code> chooses the partition, so the readings of one sensor are handled in order. Here each reading is saved with <code>nexo.db</code>.</p>
{{test:stream/sensors.nexo}}

  <h3 id="stream-processes">Producer and consumer</h3>
  <p>A program with <code>when</code> handlers keeps reading until it is stopped. It saves the position it reached, so a second run reads only new messages. One program sends and another, in another terminal, reads:</p>
{{src:stream/producer.nexo}}
{{src:stream/consumer.nexo}}
{{steps:stream/producer.nexo; ~3 stream/consumer.nexo; ~3 stream/consumer.nexo}}
  <p>The second run of the consumer prints nothing new: the three orders were acknowledged. The same two programs work on Kafka, RabbitMQ or a cloud queue by adding the broker's option to <code>kafka("orders")</code>.</p>
</section>

